97
00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:43.520
Andrew Bale: There was a time in, as Eric's slide showed, when Eric… when Alice called Bob and it just worked, and now we're in a world of pain, where communications is far too rich and sophisticated and very often just doesn't work.

98
00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:58.739
Andrew Bale: But also, it's a world where actually all of the norms that we used to have back in, really, in the 80s, before deregulation and the move to IP broke the whole, world of trusted communications.

99
00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:21.160
Andrew Bale: And our intention is to bring back that level of trust that used to be caused by both a closed garden, where you could be sure that the person whose phone was calling you was on the same network as your phone, and the ability for information to be presented securely across the network.

100
00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:31.500
Andrew Bale: The problem we face today in telecoms is that we are at the heart of a significant impact on the citizen.

101
00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:52.650
Andrew Bale: According to all the latest research, you know, there's about a trillion dollars in fraud on the consumer, happens on an annual basis, and a significant proportion of this is driven by, or enabled by, telecoms. For example, in the UK, about half of advanced payment push fraud started with a call or a text.

102
00:11:52.830 --> 00:12:07.650
Andrew Bale: Nearly half of the global population encounters a scam attempt every week, and it's essentially trivial today for a bad actor to impersonate another act… another person in a phone call.

103
00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:17.400
Andrew Bale: And this is not the peak of the problem. It's only the beginning of the problem. The cost of fraud is about to drop to effectively zero.

104
00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:32.469
Andrew Bale: Fraud is no longer kind of an artisanal activity, it's an industrial activity. And calling party impersonation, which is what we're really focused on here, is a trillion dollar problem.

105
00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:53.640
Andrew Bale: We are a small consultancy company. Tim and I work together under the aegis of Rich Connections. We've been working for many years as executives in the telecoms industry in various places. And about a year ago, we took to the GSMA.

106
00:12:54.120 --> 00:13:10.950
Andrew Bale: a proposal that we fundamentally reshape how the voice network works around decentralized identity. And the GSMA, who, as many of us know, are the foundational technology-setting body for the mobile industry.

107
00:13:10.950 --> 00:13:19.559
Andrew Bale: Agreed with our proposal. And we established, first of all, what's called a foundry project, which essentially is a community project of the willing.

108
00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:42.779
Andrew Bale: Where we demonstrated, what we're about to show you here, open verifiable calling. We built a pilot platform, and at MWC Mobile World Congress a couple of weeks ago, we showed the first, the first fruits of that project, which was a true end-to-end verifiable call.

109
00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:52.489
Andrew Bale: The Foundry project had a number of goals. The first of all, which is to prevent the impersonation that we see globally in the voice network.

110
00:13:52.540 --> 00:14:12.480
Andrew Bale: At the same time, to ensure legitimate number use. It's sort of the other side of the same problem. How do you ensure that a party who has been granted the right to use a phone number can, prove to the terminating party that they are the person with the rights to use that number?

111
00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:20.770
Andrew Bale: Part of this is about improving the experience for the citizen. Part of it is also about enabling enforcement.

112
00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:34.410
Andrew Bale: In telecoms, it's a terribly difficult problem to track down a bad actor. Bad actors can move very quickly, bad actors can be very difficult to trace

113
00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:41.020
Andrew Bale: from the complex inter-carrier network of communications that underpins the modern PSTN.

114
00:14:41.030 --> 00:14:51.810
Andrew Bale: And we wanted to build a solution that enabled much faster response by enforcement bodies to, provably, bad traffic.

115
00:14:53.210 --> 00:15:04.290
Andrew Bale: Ultimately, our goal is to protect the citizen, but to do so without breaking legitimate services. Very often, when regulators, for instance, start to impose

116
00:15:04.290 --> 00:15:18.009
Andrew Bale: actions that they want telecoms operators to undertake. The result might occasionally improve service for the citizen, but very often it also breaks legitimate use of numbering assets and services.

117
00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:35.290
Andrew Bale: So we wanted to build a system that was transparent and could support good actors, as well as keep bad actors out. And we wanted to do it through decentralized identity, which, as you'll see, is the magic ingredient from a public communications network point of view.

118
00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:44.020
Andrew Bale: So, what is the problem in the PSTN? The problem really is that trust has broken down

119
00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:54.340
Andrew Bale: Because there is no way of passing identity from an originating carrier to a terminating carrier. All that passes along this…

120
00:15:54.340 --> 00:16:04.399
Andrew Bale: piece of spaghetti between the two carriers is minimal information. You can simply guarantee that what gets to the other end is an indication of

121
00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:09.650
Andrew Bale: Of, a guess at the originating phone number.

122
00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:17.309
Andrew Bale: Some good definition of the phone number they're trying to call, and probably a time of day at which the call was originated.

123
00:16:17.810 --> 00:16:26.759
Andrew Bale: But we don't know who is making the call, and it's effectively trivial for any party to fake the originating phone number.

124
00:16:26.930 --> 00:16:34.680
Andrew Bale: Now, originating carriers, where the call actually comes from, have quite a lot of controls over what traffic they're sending.

125
00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:46.770
Andrew Bale: They often have quite a lot of regulatory requirements that they need to fulfill. If they want to give an originator a phone number, they have to KYC, KYB that customer.

126
00:16:46.890 --> 00:17:11.130
Andrew Bale: In some cases, they do have to do some form of cryptographic proof of the rights of the originating party. In the US and two other countries around the world, there is an X509-based service called Stir and Shaken that allows the originating party to attest some basic information about the originating number. Not much more than, yes, I gave this number to the

127
00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:17.700
Andrew Bale: person who's using it, not going to tell you anything about the person, but I'm confident that they're allowed to use this number.

128
00:17:18.140 --> 00:17:29.099
Andrew Bale: And then there are a whole set of other controls that they are supposed to implement, and very often don't implement, to do with keeping control over phone numbers that they allocate.

129
00:17:29.780 --> 00:17:46.809
Andrew Bale: But the problem is that almost all of that information disappears immediately the call leaves my network. Implicitly or explicitly, what is gone from the originating network does not arrive at the terminating network. And yet.

130
00:17:46.810 --> 00:17:51.620
Andrew Bale: The terminating network carrier is the guy who has the obligation to protect from harm.

131
00:17:51.860 --> 00:18:02.380
Andrew Bale: the terminating carrier is supposed to look after their customer. They're supposed to not let bad actors and bad phone numbers onto their customers' handsets.

132
00:18:02.600 --> 00:18:10.320
Andrew Bale: And yet they have no information that comes with that call, and so they have to guess at whether this is a bad actor.

133
00:18:10.320 --> 00:18:30.910
Andrew Bale: And, they have to go through ugly analytic processes to try and bash down bad actors, determine what they do and what they look like. They look at call volumes, call routing, historical behavior, analytics, reputation systems, and they make a guess as to whether this call is likely to be a bad call or not.

134
00:18:30.940 --> 00:18:31.620
Andrew Bale: not.

135
00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:54.720
Andrew Bale: Now, all of us, you know, around this call, are familiar with receiving information on my handset, which purports to tell me that this is a likely spam call, or purports to tell me that this call comes from my bank. And very few of us are aware of just how deeply that information is flawed.

136
00:18:55.070 --> 00:19:05.580
Andrew Bale: How little that information is based on evidence, and how much of that information is based on guesswork derived from, questionable sources.

137
00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:22.760
Andrew Bale: And so we fail in protecting citizens from impersonation fraud on phone networks, because the evidence that is available on the origination side has to get turned into inference on the terminating side.

138
00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:43.360
Andrew Bale: There is no mechanism for passing the evidence across. Our objective in open, verifiable communications is to restore trust by standardizing the evidence that should be gathered by an originating party, and presenting mechanisms by which that evidence can be passed and made available.

139
00:19:43.360 --> 00:19:48.379
Andrew Bale: To the terminating carrier, and ultimately to the handset of the person being called.

140
00:19:48.860 --> 00:20:06.170
Andrew Bale: So, open verifiable Calling, now rebranded as Open Verifiable Communications, because, as you'll see, our next step is to do this not just for voice, but for every part of the communications network, is to generate evidence that travels with the message.

